- `DateTime.Now` — 1 use (receipt string, line 25 — also a consistency bug; mixing `Now`/`UtcNow`)

---

## Recommendation: Use `TimeProvider` (.NET 8+)

.NET 8 ships `System.TimeProvider` — an abstract class designed exactly for this:

```csharp
// 1. Inject TimeProvider into your services
public class OrderProcessor(TimeProvider clock)
{
    public Order CreateOrder(...)
    {
        CreatedAt = clock.GetUtcNow().UtcDateTime,
        ExpiresAt = clock.GetUtcNow().UtcDateTime.AddDays(30)
        // ...
    }

    public bool IsExpired(Order order)
        => clock.GetUtcNow() > order.ExpiresAt;
}
```

```csharp
// 2. Register in DI
services.AddSingleton(TimeProvider.System); // production
```

```csharp
// 3. In tests, use the built-in fake
var fake = new Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider(
    new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));

var processor = new OrderProcessor(fake);
fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(31)); // deterministic!
Assert.True(processor.IsExpired(order));
```

Add the test package: `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing`.

If you're on an older .NET, create a simple `IClock` interface with a `UtcNow` property and inject that instead. Want me to refactor the files?
